---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import TrustPill from '../components/TrustPill.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="About Latch Tools — Private, browser-based file tools"
  description="Latch Tools is a set of free redaction and file tools that run in your browser, so your files never leave your device. No accounts and no tracking."
  ogImage="/og/about.png"
>
  <Header />

  <main>
    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="section" style="padding-bottom: 0">
      <div class="wrap" style="max-width: 780px">
        <span class="eyebrow">About</span>
        <h1 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: -.035em; line-height: 1.06; font-size: clamp(34px, 5.5vw, 54px); margin: 20px 0 0; text-wrap: balance;">
          Built to keep your<br />
          <span style="color: var(--accent)">files yours.</span>
        </h1>
        <p style="font-size: clamp(16px, 2.2vw, 18px); color: var(--text-2); max-width: 34em; margin: 20px 0 28px; text-wrap: pretty;">
          Latch is a set of free tools for redacting documents and photos, plus the everyday
          image converters people keep needing. Everything runs in your browser, so no file
          ever touches a server.
        </p>
        <TrustPill />
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Why section -->
    <section class="section">
      <div class="wrap" style="max-width: 780px">
        <h2 class="sec-title" style="margin-bottom: 28px">Why on-device?</h2>
        <div class="steps" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr)">
          <div class="step">
            <h3>Nothing to leak</h3>
            <p>A server that never receives your file can't leak it, sell it, or hand it to anyone. No privacy policy can promise as much.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <h3>Fast by default</h3>
            <p>With no upload and no queue, processing starts the moment you drop a file and often finishes in under a second. Nothing has to come back from a remote server.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <h3>Works offline</h3>
            <p>Once the page loads, Latch has everything it needs. Your files keep converting on a patchy connection, or with no connection at all.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Who section -->
    <section class="band section">
      <div class="wrap" style="max-width: 780px">
        <h2 class="sec-title" style="margin-bottom: 20px">Who's behind this?</h2>
        <p style="color: var(--text-2); font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.7; max-width: 60ch; margin: 0 0 24px;">
          Latch is an independent project. It started out of frustration with file converter
          sites that ask you to upload photos, passports, and documents to servers you know
          nothing about. The fix seemed obvious: do the work in the browser instead.
        </p>
        <p style="color: var(--text-2); font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.7; max-width: 60ch; margin: 0 0 32px;">
          Most of the site is now about redaction: blacking out text in PDFs, flattening PDFs,
          and blacking out, blurring or pixelating parts of photos, with automatic face finding
          when you blur faces. You can also strip photo metadata. The original converters are still
          here for HEIC, WebP, AVIF, JPEG XL, JFIF and SVG files, along with Base64 tools and a
          passport-photo resizer. The code is open source on
          <a href="https://github.com/TheImplementer/latch-tools" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--accent-text);font-weight:600">GitHub</a>.
        </p>
        <a href="/#tools" class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">
          Try the tools
          <svg width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M5 12h14M12 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
